son skipping two televised debates, could narrow a gap the newest poll already puts at just two points.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Laura Kelly (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Kansas is open-seat territory this year: Laura Kelly is term-limited, and the state's Republican lean returns to the foreground. Ty Masterson, the Senate's top Republican, leads Democrat Cindy Holscher, a Johnson County legislator, in every September poll, with recent surveys putting him up between two and eight points. Independent voters have broken toward Masterson so far, even as the same electorate splits in the Senate race. Five of six race raters call the contest Lean Republican, and prediction markets give Holscher roughly a three-in-ten chance.\n\nThe Democratic hope rests on a favorable national environment, Kelly's two wins as proof of a winning coalition, and a late push on property taxes and Masterson's debate absences. Skipping two televised debates has given Holscher a line of attack, but polling has barely moved. The race remains competitive but tilts Republican heading into October.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1817, "margin": -4.47}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.161, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1643, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1648, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1797, "margin": -4.8}]}